// D1371 — `a as? i32` on a HOLE parameter, delivered as an ARGUMENT.
//
// THE PROBE'S ORIGINAL PROGRAM WAS `id(a as? i32)` INTO `function id(x: i32)`, AND IT IS
// ILL-TYPED. `as?` yields `T | null` as an expression (`docs/error-handling-design.md` §Trio),
// so that program hands `i32 | null` to an `i32` parameter — which the annotated
// `function take(a: i32 | null)` twin has always refused with
// `argument 1: expected i32, got i32 | null`. It could never print 5, and a probe whose
// contract is unreachable measures nothing. The hole face now carries that same diagnostic at
// the same position, so the two faces agree.
//
// WHAT THE POSITION ACTUALLY OWED IS THIS PROGRAM: the same cast, the same argument delivery,
// with a destination the trio's own rule admits. It was check-clean invalid wasm — the `??`
// FOLDED, because `Paren` banks a copy of its inner's checked type and the re-record at the
// pin lands on the inner node only, so the un-parenthesised `id(a as? i32 ?? 0)` ran and this
// one did not. Both spellings run now.
// Should print 5.
function id(x: i32): i32 { return x }
const u: i32 | null = 5

function take(a) { print(id((a as? i32) ?? 0)) }

take(u)
